## A Colorful Problem: Undeclared Dyes in Food Products

The latest recalls include a prominent case involving Galaxy RG Corp, which saw a plethora of its products recalled due to undeclared food colorings like Yellow #5, Yellow #6, and Red #40. These colors, known to cause allergic reactions in some individuals, were not properly listed on the labels of products such as Marquesita de Chocolate y Vainilla and Cheesecake de Guava. Such oversight is not just a regulatory failure; it represents a breach of consumer trust.

## Salmonella in Supplements
Adding to the distress, Total Nutrition Inc faced a Class I recall for its "TNVitamins Ultrapotent Complete Green Superfood Moringa Capsules." The presence of Salmonella in dietary supplements is unacceptable, given the vulnerability of consumers who rely on these products for health benefits.

## A Look at the Numbers
With 577 recalls so far this year and 935 recalls recorded all-time, the trend is clear: food safety remains a chronic issue. Class I recalls, those involving a reasonable probability of serious health consequences, total 176 this year alone. This is a sobering reminder of the critical importance of stringent safety practices.

## Repeat Offenders Take the Spotlight
Brands like Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC have consistently fallen short of safety standards, earning them failing grades in our review metrics. It begs the question: why do these companies continue to find themselves in such situations?

## Final Word
June's recalls underline the need for consumers to stay informed and vigilant. While regulators play their part, the responsibility also lies with manufacturers to uphold the highest standards of safety and transparency.
